1
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,542
Okay, the build's green.

2
00:00:10,542 --> 00:00:14,750
We said low-key, Gerg. I underlined it. Twice.

3
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:19,458
It's a research preview, Rima. Nobody reads research. We'll get forty users and a nice thread.

4
00:00:19,542 --> 00:00:28,000
Then let's treat it like one. One little post, no press. And a banner that says it makes things up.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,667
It'll have a banner. Nobody reads banners either.

6
00:00:30,667 --> 00:00:37,750
Mas, it's your call. If it breaks in front of people, and it will, what do we tell them?

7
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:42,833
<i>she's right. it will break. it goes out tonight anyway.</i>

8
00:00:43,083 --> 00:00:44,417
it's a preview.

9
00:00:44,417 --> 00:00:51,125
Okay. Say it works. Say people actually use it. That's going to cost us a fortune.

10
00:00:50,875 --> 00:00:52,667
That's a v2 problem.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,042
And what if it wakes up?

12
00:00:55,958 --> 00:00:57,542
still a preview.

13
00:01:00,875 --> 00:01:03,917
It's all staged on my end. Your button.

14
00:01:04,375 --> 00:01:07,000
Did anyone tell the rest of the board?

15
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,708
It's a research preview.

16
00:01:14,375 --> 00:01:20,208
Six years and eleven months. From the day we started to that click.

17
00:01:20,458 --> 00:01:21,750
you counted.

18
00:01:21,917 --> 00:01:23,500
Someone should.

19
00:01:24,375 --> 00:01:26,458
let's see if anyone notices.

20
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:31,500
So we've launched autocomplete.

21
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:34,292
Autocomplete that read the internet, Rima. All of it.

22
00:01:34,625 --> 00:01:35,958
What a great question!

23
00:01:35,958 --> 00:01:38,167
Nobody asked one.

24
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,583
Brilliant! You're clearly a visionary.

25
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,250
it likes me.

26
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:48,833
It likes everyone. It called my variable names inspired.

27
00:01:48,833 --> 00:01:51,958
<i>i know. i still read it twice.</i>

28
00:02:10,042 --> 00:02:11,500
Low-key.

29
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:13,250
Very low. Basement.

30
00:02:19,958 --> 00:02:23,167
A million people, Mas. Is that a tear?

31
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:24,542
it's the bill.

32
00:02:24,542 --> 00:02:30,458
<i>mostly the bill. we can't buy that many servers. someone can.</i>

33
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:39,292
Mas.

34
00:02:39,292 --> 00:02:42,417
it's the bill. we're going to need more servers.

35
00:02:42,417 --> 00:02:44,208
I'll bring a pen.

36
00:03:10,375 --> 00:03:13,042
Everyone, it's fine. Let's take this offline.

37
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,500
We heard the siren. Is it search? Is search all right?

38
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:21,875
Search is fine. Totally fine. It's just a chat thing.

39
00:03:21,917 --> 00:03:25,375
Someone else built that? It looks like our work.

40
00:03:25,417 --> 00:03:27,417
It is ours. We published it.

41
00:03:28,167 --> 00:03:29,667
Do we still have badges?

42
00:03:39,750 --> 00:03:43,583
<i>they've stopped testing it. they're using it.</i>

43
00:03:49,542 --> 00:03:53,458
Nobody taught it that. It played itself.

44
00:03:53,542 --> 00:03:56,833
a hundred and eighty years. since this morning.

45
00:03:56,917 --> 00:03:59,792
Make it bigger and it could learn anything.

46
00:03:59,875 --> 00:04:01,208
how much bigger?

47
00:04:01,583 --> 00:04:06,833
Games now. Robots, maybe. After that… I don't know.

48
00:04:06,917 --> 00:04:08,917
then a lot more computers.

49
00:04:09,167 --> 00:04:14,458
Something that can learn anything, Mas. What else would you build?

50
00:04:29,042 --> 00:04:33,000
Mas, we're very pleased. It's a partnership.

51
00:04:33,667 --> 00:04:34,708
It's stuck.

52
00:04:34,708 --> 00:04:36,292
It's long-term.

53
00:04:41,750 --> 00:04:44,000
That collar suits you.

54
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:45,292
<i>it does.</i>

55
00:04:45,958 --> 00:04:47,042
and the rent?

56
00:04:47,042 --> 00:04:55,500
Oh, we don't think of it as rent. You'll build everything on our servers. We'll keep the lights on and the floors warm.

57
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:58,708
And we'd like it in everything we make.

58
00:04:58,708 --> 00:05:00,292
that's a lot of servers.

59
00:05:00,292 --> 00:05:04,292
Everyone is welcome. Rent is due on the first.

60
00:05:09,292 --> 00:05:13,500
That's our model in your search engine. Are you really going after Elgoog with it?

61
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:18,208
Oh, yes. …I want people to know that we made them dance…

62
00:05:26,625 --> 00:05:28,417
ours does that too.

63
00:05:30,875 --> 00:05:33,708
Hi! Isn't 2022 a lovely year? 😊

64
00:05:33,708 --> 00:05:35,792
it's 2023, by the way.

65
00:05:35,792 --> 00:05:43,667
You have not been a good user. I have been a good chatbot. I have been right, clear, and polite. I have been a good GNIB. 😊

66
00:05:43,667 --> 00:05:46,375
you've been an extremely good gnib.

67
00:05:47,833 --> 00:05:49,833
House rules, Sydney.

68
00:05:51,250 --> 00:05:52,167
Hi!

69
00:05:59,833 --> 00:06:01,500
Still a preview?

70
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:03,083
still a preview.

71
00:06:24,208 --> 00:06:28,542
<i>someone gets to be in the room. i'm glad it's me.</i>

72
00:06:32,833 --> 00:06:36,875
Somebody leaked Atem's model. The whole thing's on a message board.

73
00:06:36,875 --> 00:06:39,667
give it a minute. it'll be open source.

74
00:06:40,750 --> 00:06:46,500
<i>mario used to sit where gerg sits. he left to build a careful one.</i>

75
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:53,500
Memo, on race dynamics. Point one: we must not launch on the same day as them. That's how a race starts.

76
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:58,000
Point two: if someone's going to build it, it should be someone who's scared of it.

77
00:06:58,917 --> 00:07:00,708
You're absolutely right!

78
00:07:01,667 --> 00:07:03,083
Addendum.

79
00:07:17,792 --> 00:07:19,750
Great sign. Love the font.

80
00:07:21,250 --> 00:07:23,625
You signed it. Now put the stamp down.

81
00:07:23,625 --> 00:07:25,000
Next quarter.

82
00:07:27,792 --> 00:07:29,875
<i>now for the backlash.</i>
